WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:04.060
- Deadlines, we all face them every day,

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:06.450
things to do, places to be.

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:09.760
Since 2016, the UK has been obsessed

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:11.509
with one deadline in particular.

00:00:11.509 --> 00:00:12.609
- Brexit.
- Brexit.

00:00:12.609 --> 00:00:13.860
- Brexit.
- Brexit.

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:15.277
- Brexit, Brexit.

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:17.133
- [Jason] It's a deadline

00:00:17.133 --> 00:00:19.280
that's been pushed back several times.

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:21.820
- We're leaving on the 29th of March.

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:23.860
- On October the 31st.

00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:25.595
- By January the 31st.

00:00:25.595 --> 00:00:28.270
- Now, after three and a
half years of negotiations,

00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:29.750
it's finally happening.

00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:31.900
- We are leaving the European Union.

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:34.270
- But although the UK is officially out,

00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:36.650
it now enters a transition period

00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:39.420
which comes with its own deadlines.

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:42.080
- This is not an end, but a beginning.

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:43.920
- Now the tricky part starts.

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:46.130
By the end of 2020, Britain and the EU

00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:48.890
have to work out a whole new relationship

00:00:48.890 --> 00:00:51.700
covering everything from finance to fish,

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:53.530
and UK Prime Minister Boris Johnson

00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:56.380
has said he won't extend that deadline,

00:00:56.380 --> 00:00:58.400
but many analysts are already saying

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:01.840
that, again, that deadline
could be difficult to meet,

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:04.370
and rushing things
could potentially result

00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:07.270
in a messy, hasty separation.

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:09.980
So when will Britain
really be done with Brexit?

00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:12.340
If the past three years
are anything to go by,

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:15.330
it could be hard to tell.
(pleasant orchestral music)

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:17.580
Britain's first
self-imposed Brexit deadline

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:19.870
was set in 2016.

00:01:19.870 --> 00:01:21.800
- Through an in-out referendum

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:24.240
on Thursday, the 23rd of June.

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:25.760
- [Jason] Cameron thought
that setting a deadline

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:27.470
would force people to choose

00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:30.350
so the country and his
party could move on.

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:33.630
Of course, we know the vote
didn't go the way he wanted.

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:37.170
- The British people have voted
to leave the European Union.

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:38.760
- [Jason] But that didn't
stop his successors

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:43.043
from also using deadlines as
a way to coerce consensus.

00:01:43.043 --> 00:01:44.407
- There will be no unnecessary delays.

00:01:44.407 --> 00:01:48.140
And there can be no turning back.

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:51.973
- We must leave the EU
on October the 31st.

00:01:52.878 --> 00:01:55.000
- And if there's one thing
we've learned from Brexit,

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:57.043
it's that deadlines don't always stick.

00:01:57.890 --> 00:02:01.290
- The United Kingdom is
leaving the European Union.

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:02.470
- [Jason] Prime Minister Theresa May

00:02:02.470 --> 00:02:05.130
lacked the support in Parliament
to get the Brexit deal

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:07.470
she negotiated passed into law,

00:02:07.470 --> 00:02:11.320
forcing her to go back to
the EU and ask for extension.

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:13.850
- We will need a further
extension of Article 50.

00:02:13.850 --> 00:02:15.050
- [Jason] After extension.

00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:17.260
- I agreed an extension
to the Brexit process

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:19.600
to the end of October at the latest.

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:22.400
- Showing that, without the
backing of the UK Parliament,

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:24.920
those deadlines don't mean very much.

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:26.470
That's where Johnson comes in.

00:02:26.470 --> 00:02:29.950
- And come out of the
EU on October the 31st,

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:31.860
no ifs or buts.

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:33.907
- [Jason] Johnson replaced
Theresa May in July,

00:02:33.907 --> 00:02:35.720
and the first thing he did,

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:37.910
following up on a key campaign promise,

00:02:37.910 --> 00:02:39.540
was to enforce that deadline.

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:41.350
- [Questioner] Can you
make a promise today

00:02:41.350 --> 00:02:44.570
to the British public that you
will not go back to Brussels

00:02:44.570 --> 00:02:46.567
and ask for another delay to Brexit?

00:02:46.567 --> 00:02:49.400
- Yes, I can.
- And, would you be--

00:02:50.410 --> 00:02:52.221
- I'd rather be dead in a ditch.

00:02:52.221 --> 00:02:53.770
- [Jason] But again, that didn't happen.

00:02:53.770 --> 00:02:54.950
Just like Theresa May,

00:02:54.950 --> 00:02:57.760
it was because he had no
majority in Parliament.

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:00.280
Lawmakers forced him to
send a letter to Brussels

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:01.740
requesting an extension.

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:04.510
The new deadline was set for January 31st.

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:06.200
His threat of a no-deal exit,

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:08.130
with Britain crashing out of the bloc,

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:10.250
was neutralized by Parliament.

00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:11.510
His solution?

00:03:11.510 --> 00:03:13.410
To call for a snap election.

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:17.200
- Vote for an election
and let the people decide.

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:19.320
- Only after winning big at that election

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:21.380
was he able to ensure his Brexit deal

00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:22.970
got through Parliament
(group shouting)

00:03:22.970 --> 00:03:26.870
and, this time, finally meet
that deadline of January 31st.

00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:29.460
There are now two
deadlines on the horizon.

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:30.820
The UK's transition period

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:33.400
with the EU ends on December the 31st,

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:34.690
and if it needs an extension,

00:03:34.690 --> 00:03:37.770
it needs to ask the EU by July the 1st.

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:40.440
Analysts have said that
it is likely the UK

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:44.550
will be only partially ready
by the end of December 2020.

00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:47.080
Just like during the Brexit negotiation,

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:49.200
some EU leaders are already talking

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:51.300
about a possible extension period.

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:56.250
- Without an extension of the
transition period beyond 2020,

00:03:56.250 --> 00:04:00.830
you cannot expect to agree
on every single aspect

00:04:00.830 --> 00:04:02.480
of our new partnership.

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:04.090
- Johnson's strong majority in Parliament

00:04:04.090 --> 00:04:05.030
means that lawmakers

00:04:05.030 --> 00:04:08.060
won't cause him to miss another deadline,

00:04:08.060 --> 00:04:09.810
but he may find that he's left himself

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:12.750
too much to negotiate
before the end of the year

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:14.740
and decide to postpone.

00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:17.070
If recent experience is any guide,

00:04:17.070 --> 00:04:19.961
the UK's Brexit drama
could be far from over.

00:04:19.961 --> 00:04:23.264
(dramatic orchestral music)

